+// DLIO integrates IMU samples using dt = header.stamp(N) - header.stamp(N-1) (see
+// odom.cc's IMU callback). Under PX4 lockstep + this host's dual-drone load, Gazebo's
+// achieved real-time-factor sits sustained around 0.96-0.97 (measured live via `gz
+// stats`: ~43s of sim-vs-wall drift accumulated after ~400s of runtime) -- so a
+// wall-clock dt is systematically ~3-10% larger than the sim time actually elapsed
+// for the motion that produced each accel/gyro sample, and that bias compounds every
+// integration step. This is very likely the dominant contributor to this project's
+// long-standing "diverges to thousands of meters while stationary" DLIO bug.
+//
+// Switching straight to Gazebo sim time (sensor_->LastUpdateTime()) is *not* safe on
+// its own: the matching fix in livox_points_plugin.cpp documents that DLIO's own
+// getScanFromROS() autodetects Livox-vs-Hesai point clouds by checking whether the
+// raw timestamp exceeds ~1e14ns (a real absolute Unix epoch is that large; "seconds
+// since sim start" is not) -- a small sim-time value there gets misclassified as
+// Hesai and silently freezes deskewing forever (see livox_pointcloud_timestamp.patch's
+// comment for the full story). Offsetting sim time by a fixed large constant keeps it
+// looking like a plausible absolute epoch (satisfies that >1e14ns heuristic) while
+// keeping every *delta* between consecutive stamps exactly equal to true elapsed sim
+// time -- immune to real-time-factor drift. The lidar plugin uses the identical
+// offset so the IMU and point cloud stay on one consistent clock, which is what
+// deskewing's IMU-buffer bracket search (integrateImu()) requires.
+constexpr int64_t kFakeEpochOffsetNs = 1735689600LL * 1000000000LL;  // 2025-01-01T00:00:00Z
+
+builtin_interfaces::msg::Time SimTimeAsFakeEpoch(const gazebo::common::Time & sim_time)
+{
+  int64_t sim_ns = static_cast<int64_t>(sim_time.sec) * 1000000000LL +
+    static_cast<int64_t>(sim_time.nsec) + kFakeEpochOffsetNs;
+  builtin_interfaces::msg::Time t;
+  t.sec = static_cast<int32_t>(sim_ns / 1000000000LL);
+  t.nanosec = static_cast<uint32_t>(sim_ns % 1000000000LL);
+  return t;
+}
+}  // namespace
